The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
__aa_profile_list_release(&profile->base.profiles);
```
Reason:  
This function recursively removes all nested profiles. With a deep hierarchy (e.g., 1024 nested profiles), each recursion consumes kernel stack space, leading to stack exhaustion and a system crash.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
if (!list_empty(&profile->base.profiles)) {
    curr = list_first_entry(&profile->base.profiles, struct aa_profile, base.list);
    while (curr != profile) {
        while (!list_empty(&curr->base.profiles))
            curr = list_first_entry(&curr->base.profiles, struct aa_profile, base.list);
        to_remove = curr;
        if (!list_is_last(&to_remove->base.list, &aa_deref_parent(curr)->base.profiles))
            curr = list_next_entry(to_remove, base.list);
        else
            curr = aa_deref_parent(curr);
        aa_label_remove(&to_remove->label);
        __aafs_profile_rmdir(to_remove);
        __list_remove_profile(to_remove);
    }
}
```
Reason:  
This iterative approach repeatedly finds and removes the deepest leaf profile, then moves to the next sibling or parent, eliminating all recursion. It maintains the same removal semantics while preventing stack exhaustion.

